## Size and rhythm

Prose reads the global type controls, so retuning `--typeset-base-*`, `--typeset-scale-*`, or `--paragraph-spacing-multiplier` on `:root` changes every article. To size one article differently, give it a preset:

- `data-prose-size="sm"`: compact. 0.875 times the base, leading 1.5, and three quarters of the space between blocks. For dense docs and sidebars.
- `data-prose-size="lg"`: reading. 1.125 times the base, leading 1.8, and a quarter more space between blocks. For long-form articles.

A preset scales the base and recomputes the type scale for that article, so its headings, body text, and roles grow or shrink together, and your `:root` controls still apply. Kit components inside it read the same tokens, so they scale too. `data-ui="not-prose"` stops the prose margins on a component but not its size; set its size utility to pin that.

`--ui-prose-flow` multiplies the space between blocks without touching the type, on `:root` for every article or on one: `style="--ui-prose-flow: 1.5"`. It stacks on the preset's spacing and the global `--paragraph-spacing-multiplier`.

```html
<article data-ui="prose" data-prose-size="lg" style="--ui-prose-flow: 1.5">
  <h2>Roomy article</h2>
  <p>Larger text, looser leading, and the preset's spacing half again.</p>
</article>
```

## Opting out

Prose styles everything inside it. To embed a widget in an article (a form, a menu, a demo) without prose margins and list styles, put `data-ui="not-prose"` on its wrapper; prose picks up again after it.

```html
<article data-ui="prose">
  <p>Prose styles this paragraph.</p>
  <div data-ui="not-prose">
    <ul><li>But not this list.</li></ul>
  </div>
</article>
```

